object CodePointRepository {
    // Stored as chunked string literals rather than one intArrayOf(...) of
    // every character's code point -- see buildKotlinSource in lawgpt.html
    // for why.
    private val textChunks: List<String> = listOf(
        "[0m[1m1. Rules[0m\n[0m[1mRule: Due process limit on state-court jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding and narrative; Goodyear Dunlop Tires\nOperations, S.A. v. Brown, pp. 1107 and 1109\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A state court's assertion of jurisdiction exposes a\ndefendant to the State's coercive power, so it is reviewed for\ncompatibility with the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process\nClause, which \"sets the outer boundaries of a state tribunal's\nauthority to proceed against a defendant\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: What sets the outer limit on a state court's\npower to proceed against an out-of-state defendant?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The exercise of jurisdiction must be compatible\nwith the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A defendant invokes this rule to contest a state\ncourt's authority over it; the court then asks whether general\nor specific jurisdiction can be exercised consistently with due\nprocess\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states no exception; it describes due\nprocess as an outer boundary and does not address any limit\nother than due process\n\n[0m[1mRule: Minimum contacts test[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; International Shoe Co. v. Washington, as\nquoted in Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, p.\n1109\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A State may authorize its courts to exercise\npersonal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant if the\ndefendant has \"certain minimum contacts with the State such that\nthe maintenance of the suit does not offend 'traditional notions\nof fair play and substantial justice'\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a State authorize its courts to\nexercise personal jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. The defendant has certain minimum contacts with the State\n    2. Maintaining the suit does not offend traditional notions\n    of fair play and substantial justice\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This is the starting framework for personal\njurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant; the Court calls\nInternational Shoe the \"canonical opinion\" and describes its\ncase categories as an effort to give specific content to \"fair\nplay and substantial justice\"\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states no exception, but it separately\nrefers to a foreign corporation that \"has not consented to suit\nin the forum,\" which suggests that consent is treated\ndifferently; the reading does not develop that point\n\n[0m[1mRule: Specific jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding and narrative; Goodyear Dunlop Tires\nOperations, S.A. v. Brown, pp. 1107 and 1109\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Specific or case-linked jurisdiction depends on an\n\"affiliation between the forum and the underlying controversy,\"\nprincipally \"activity or an occurrence that takes place in the\nforum State and is therefore subject to the State's regulation\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a forum hear a claim because that\nclaim is connected to the forum?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: There must be an affiliation between the forum\nand the underlying controversy, principally in-forum activity or\nan in-forum occurrence\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The court looks at where the episode-in-suit\nhappened and how the claim connects to the forum; here, the\naccident occurred in France and the tire was manufactured and\nsold abroad, so North Carolina lacked specific jurisdiction;\nunlike general jurisdiction, this inquiry is tied to the\nparticular claim\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Jurisdiction based on \"single or occasional acts\"\nreaches only claims connected to those acts, not \"matters\nunrelated to the forum connections\"\n\n[0m[1mRule: Categories of specific jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; International Shoe Co. v. Washington, as\nclassified in Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown,\np. 1109\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Specific jurisdiction covers cases where a\ncorporation's in-state activity is \"continuous and systematic\"\nand gave rise to the episode-in-suit, and cases where \"single or\noccasional acts\" in the State may be sufficient to make the\ncorporation answerable with respect to those acts\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Which patterns of corporate in-state activity\nfall under the heading of specific jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Either of the following:\n    1. Continuous and systematic in-state activity that gave\n    rise to the episode-in-suit\n    2. Certain single or occasional in-state acts, with the suit\n    concerning those acts\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use this rule to sort a defendant's contacts by\nvolume and by connection to the claim; \"continuous and\nsystematic\" activity appears in both specific and general\njurisdiction, so the phrase alone does not decide the category,\nand the key questions are whether the activity gave rise to the\nsuit and, if not, whether the corporation is at home in the\nforum\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: Only \"certain\" single or occasional acts qualify,\nand they \"may be\" sufficient, so a single in-state act does not\nautomatically support jurisdiction\n\n[0m[1mRule: Purposeful availment[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Hanson v. Denckla, as quoted in Goodyear\nDunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, p. 1110\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: As a rule in specific-jurisdiction cases involving\nsingle or occasional acts, the Court has asked whether there was\n\"some act by which the defendant purposefully availed itself of\nthe privilege of conducting activities within the forum State,\nthus invoking the benefits and protections of its laws\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: In a specific-jurisdiction case based on\nsingle or occasional acts, did the defendant's own conduct\nconnect it to the forum in a way that supports jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m:\n    1. Some act by the defendant\n    2. Purposeful availment of the privilege of conducting\n    activities within the forum State\n    3. Invocation of the benefits and protections of the forum's\n    laws\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: The inquiry is used in specific-jurisdiction\ndisputes over acts occurring or having their impact in the\nforum; Goodyear describes it as part of the\nspecific-jurisdiction case law and does not use it to decide\ngeneral jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The Court frames this as the inquiry \"as a rule\"\nin these cases and states no specific exception\n\n[0m[1mRule: General jurisdiction at-home standard[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, p. 1107\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A court may hear \"any and all claims\" against\nsister-state or foreign-country corporations when their\naffiliations with the State are \"so 'continuous and systematic'\nas to render them essentially at home in the forum State\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: When may a state court hear claims against an\nout-of-state corporation that are unrelated to its forum\ncontacts?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The corporation's affiliations with the State\nmust be so continuous and systematic that the corporation is\nessentially at home there\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: This rule applies when the claim does not arise\nfrom forum activity, so the plaintiff must show that the\ncorporation is at home in the forum; the focus is on the\ncorporation's overall affiliation with the State, not on the\nevent in suit\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: \"Continuous activity of some sorts within a state\"\nis not enough, and a limited connection through products\nreaching the forum is inadequate\n\n[0m[1mRule: Paradigm forums for general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case narrative; Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, p. 1110, citing Brilmayer, Haverkamp, and Logan, 66\nTexas L. Rev. 721, 728\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The paradigm forum for general jurisdiction over an\nindividual is the individual's domicile, and for a corporation\nit is \"an equivalent place, one in which the corporation is\nfairly regarded as at home\"; the cited article identifies\ndomicile, place of incorporation, and principal place of\nbusiness as paradigm bases\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Where is a defendant ordinarily subject to\ngeneral jurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: Either of the following:\n    1. For an individual, the forum is the individual's domicile\n    2. For a corporation, the forum is an equivalent place where\n    it is fairly regarded as at home\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Start the general-jurisdiction analysis by\ncomparing the forum with these paradigm places; the subsidiaries\nhere were incorporated in Luxembourg, Turkey, and France and had\nno place of business in North Carolina\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The word \"paradigm\" does not itself make the list\nexclusive; the Philippine corporation in Perkins v. Benguet\nConsol. Mining Co. was subject to general jurisdiction in Ohio\non the facts described in the reading\n\n[0m[1mRule: Continuous activity insufficient for general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; International Shoe Co. v. Washington, as\nquoted in Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown, p.\n1111\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: A corporation's \"continuous activity of some sorts\nwithin a state\" is \"not enough to support the demand that the\ncorporation be amenable to suits unrelated to that activity\";\ngeneral jurisdiction instead requires corporate operations \"so\nsubstantial and of such a nature\" as to justify suit on causes\nof action \"entirely distinct from those activities\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Does ongoing in-state activity by itself\nsubject a corporation to suit on unrelated claims?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: The continuous corporate operations must be so\nsubstantial and of such a nature as to justify suit on causes of\naction arising from dealings entirely distinct from those\noperations\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Defendants use this rule to argue that regular\nforum activity is not the same as being at home in the forum,\nespecially when the claim has no connection to that activity\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The rule limits suits unrelated to the activity;\ncontinuous and systematic activity that gave rise to the\nepisode-in-suit can support specific jurisdiction\n\n[0m[1mRule: Stream of commerce and general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, pp. 1108, 1111, and 1112\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: The flow of a manufacturer's products into the\nforum \"may bolster an affiliation germane to specific\njurisdiction,\" but \"ties serving to bolster the exercise of\nspecific jurisdiction do not warrant a determination that, based\non those ties, the forum has general jurisdiction over a\ndefendant\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Can the arrival of a manufacturer's products\nin the forum through the stream of commerce support general\njurisdiction?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: A stream-of-commerce connection alone does not\nsatisfy general jurisdiction; the defendant must still have the\ncontinuous and systematic affiliation that makes it essentially\nat home\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use this rule when a plaintiff relies on product\ndistribution in the forum for a claim unrelated to those\nproducts; the court must keep the general and specific inquiries\nseparate, and the Court warned that the contrary \"sprawling\nview\" would subject any substantial manufacturer or seller to\nsuit on any claim wherever its products are distributed\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The rule does not eliminate stream-of-commerce\nevidence, because product flow may still bolster specific\njurisdiction\n\n[0m[1mRule: Forum purchases and general jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Helicopteros Nacionales de Colombia,\nS.A. v. Hall, as quoted and applied in Goodyear Dunlop Tires\nOperations, S.A. v. Brown, pp. 1111 and 1112\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: \"Mere purchases made in the forum State, even if\noccurring at regular intervals, are not enough to warrant a\nState's assertion of general jurisdiction over a nonresident\ncorporation in a cause of action not related to those purchase\ntransactions\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Do regular purchases in a forum subject a\nnonresident corporation to general jurisdiction on an unrelated\nclaim?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: To fall within this rule, the contacts must be\nmere forum purchases and the cause of action must be unrelated\nto those purchases\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use this rule to compare a defendant's commercial\nties with those held insufficient in Helicopteros; Goodyear saw\n\"no reason to differentiate\" sporadic sales of the subsidiaries'\ntires in North Carolina through intermediaries\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The holding is limited to a cause of action \"not\nrelated to those purchase transactions\"; the reading does not\ntreat it as resolving a claim that arises from the purchases\n\n[0m[1mRule: General jurisdiction at the center of corporate activity[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Perkins v. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.,\nas described in Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown,\npp. 1110 to 1112\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Due process was not violated when Ohio adjudicated\na claim that did not arise there against a Philippine\ncorporation whose wartime business activity was conducted and\nsupervised from Ohio; Goodyear calls this \"the textbook case of\ngeneral jurisdiction appropriately exercised over a foreign\ncorporation that has not consented to suit in the forum\"\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: May a forum exercise general jurisdiction over\na foreign corporation when the corporation's actual business\nactivity is run from that forum, even if the claim arose\nelsewhere?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: As inferred from the facts Goodyear emphasizes:\n    1. The corporation has not consented to suit in the forum\n    2. The corporation's business activity, to the extent it is\n    conducting any, is carried on in the forum\n    3. The forum is where the corporation's affairs are\n    overseen, as shown by its president's office, company files,\n    and supervision of activities\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: Use Perkins as the positive benchmark for general\njurisdiction; Goodyear contrasted that corporation's sole\nwartime business activity in Ohio with the subsidiaries'\nattenuated connections to North Carolina\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading ties the result to the wartime\ncircumstances and does not say that a corporate office or file\nstorage in a forum alone would be enough\n\n[0m[1mRule: Forfeiture of unraised arguments[0m\n- [0m[1mSource[0m: Case holding; Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\nv. Brown, p. 1112\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mStatement[0m: Respondents forfeited their \"single enterprise\"\ntheory by failing to urge it in the courts below or in their\nbrief in opposition to the petition for certiorari, so the\nSupreme Court did not address it\n- [0m[1mLegal Question[0m: Will the Supreme Court decide a jurisdictional\ntheory that respondents raised belatedly?\n- [0m[1mRequirements[0m: As applied in this case:\n    1. Respondents did not urge the theory below\n    2. Respondents did not urge the theory in their brief in\n    opposition to the petition for certiorari\n- [0m[1mApplication[0m: A party opposing a late-raised theory may invoke\nforfeiture to keep the court from reaching it; the disposition\nhere leaves the merits of the single-enterprise theory undecided\n- [0m[1mExceptions[0m: The reading states no exception; this is a ruling\non preservation and does not decide whether corporate veils can\nbe pierced for jurisdictional purposes\n[0m[1m2. Case Narrative[0m\n[0m[1mGoodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown[0m\nOn April 18, 2004, a bus headed to Charles de Gaulle Airport\noverturned on a road outside Paris, France. Its passengers\nincluded young soccer players from North Carolina who were\nbeginning their journey home. Two 13-year-old boys, Julian Brown\nand Matthew Helms, were fatally injured. The accident was\nattributed to a tire that failed when its plies separated. It\nwas a Goodyear Regional RHS tire manufactured in Turkey by\nGoodyear Turkey. The reading does not say who operated the bus\nor give further details about the boys' trip.\n\"Brown\" in the caption refers to the respondents' side, the\nboys' parents, who acted as administrators of their sons'\nestates and were the plaintiffs. The defendants were Goodyear\nUSA, an Ohio corporation, and three of its indirect\nsubsidiaries: Goodyear Luxembourg Tires, SA; Goodyear Lastikleri\nT.A.S. (Goodyear Turkey); and Goodyear Dunlop Tires France, SA.\nThese subsidiaries were incorporated in Luxembourg, Turkey, and\nFrance, respectively, and are the petitioners. The excerpt does\nnot separately identify an entity under the exact caption name\n\"Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A.\"\nThe companies' connections to North Carolina differed sharply.\nGoodyear USA had plants in the State and regularly did business\nthere. The subsidiaries manufactured tires primarily for\nEuropean and Asian markets, and those tires differed in size and\nconstruction from tires ordinarily sold in the United States.\nThey were designed to carry significantly heavier loads and to\nserve under the road conditions and speed limits of the\nmanufacturers' primary markets.\nThe subsidiaries were not registered to do business in North\nCarolina and had no place of business, employees, or bank\naccounts there. They did not design, manufacture, or advertise\nproducts there, solicit business there, or themselves sell or\nship tires to North Carolina customers. Even so, other Goodyear\nUSA affiliates distributed a small percentage of their tires in\nthe State: tens of thousands out of the tens of millions\nmanufactured between 2004 and 2007. These tires were typically\ncustom ordered for specialized vehicles such as cement mixers,\nwaste haulers, and boat and horse trailers. The petitioners\nstated, and the respondents did not deny in the Supreme Court,\nthat the type of tire involved in the accident was never\ndistributed in North Carolina.\nThe parents blamed the deaths on negligence in the tire's\n\"design, construction, testing, and inspection.\" They sued\nGoodyear USA and the three subsidiaries in the Superior Court of\nOnslow County, North Carolina, seeking wrongful-death damages.\n[0m[1mPerkins v. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.[0m\nThe reading describes this case only as background in Goodyear.\nBenguet was a Philippine mining corporation and the defendant in\na suit brought in Ohio. The reading does not say who Perkins\nwas, beyond the caption's indication that Perkins was on the\nopposing side, and it does not describe the claim except to say\nthat it did not arise in Ohio.\nThe relevant events took place during and immediately after\nWorld War II and the Japanese occupation of the Philippines. The\ncompany ceased its activities in the Philippines during the war.\nTo the extent that it was doing any business then, it did so in\nOhio. Its president maintained his office there, kept the\ncompany files in that office, and from there supervised \"the\nnecessarily limited wartime activities of the company.\" The\nreading does not say what went wrong between the parties. A suit\nfollowed against the corporation in Ohio, but the reading does\nnot state what relief was sought.\n[0m[1mHelicopteros Nacionales de Colombia, S.A. v. Hall[0m\nThis case also appears as background in Goodyear. Helicopteros\nwas a Colombian corporation that owned and operated a\nhelicopter. \"Hall\" is on the plaintiffs' side of the caption,\nbut the reading does not identify Hall individually. It\ndescribes the plaintiffs only as survivors of United States\ncitizens who died when the helicopter crashed in Per",
        "u. The\nreading does not give the date of the crash, the reason the\nvictims were aboard, or the cause of the crash.\nThe Colombian corporation had no place of business in Texas and\nwas not licensed to do business there. Its Texas ties consisted\nof sending its chief executive officer to Houston for a\ncontract-negotiation session, accepting into its New York bank\naccount checks drawn on a Houston bank, buying helicopters,\nequipment, and training services for substantial sums from a\nTexas enterprise, and sending personnel to Texas for training.\nAfter the crash, the survivors brought wrongful-death actions\nagainst the corporation in a Texas state court.\n[0m[1m3. Cases[0m\n[0m[1mCase Name: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - On April 18, 2004, a bus bound for Charles de Gaulle\n    Airport overturned outside Paris, killing 13-year-old North\n    Carolina soccer players Julian Brown and Matthew Helms\n    - The boys' parents, as administrators of their estates,\n    sought wrongful-death damages and alleged negligence in the\n    tire's \"design, construction, testing, and inspection\"\n    - The accident was attributed to a tire that failed when its\n    plies separated; the tire was manufactured in Turkey and,\n    according to the Court, manufactured and sold abroad\n    - The defendants were Goodyear USA, an Ohio corporation, and\n    three indirect subsidiaries incorporated in Luxembourg,\n    Turkey, and France\n    - Goodyear USA had North Carolina plants, regularly engaged\n    in commercial activity there, and did not contest\n    jurisdiction\n    - The subsidiaries were not registered in North Carolina and\n    had no place of business, employees, or bank accounts there\n    - The subsidiaries did not design, manufacture, or advertise\n    products in North Carolina, solicit business there, or\n    themselves sell or ship tires to North Carolina customers\n    - Other Goodyear USA affiliates distributed tens of\n    thousands of the subsidiaries' tires in North Carolina, out\n    of tens of millions manufactured between 2004 and 2007, and\n    these were typically custom ordered for specialized vehicles\n    - The subsidiaries' tires differed in size and construction\n    from tires ordinarily sold in the United States and were\n    made primarily for European and Asian markets\n    - The Goodyear Regional RHS tire type involved in the\n    accident was never distributed in North Carolina, as\n    petitioners stated and respondents did not deny in the\n    Supreme Court\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Superior Court of Onslow County, North Carolina: upheld\n    jurisdiction over the foreign subsidiaries on their\n    challenge to North Carolina's adjudicatory authority\n    - Appeal: Goodyear Luxembourg, Goodyear Turkey, and Goodyear\n    France (defendants below), as Defendants-Appellants, sought\n    review in the North Carolina Court of Appeals on the grounds\n    that North Carolina lacked adjudicatory authority over them\n    - North Carolina Court of Appeals: acknowledged that North\n    Carolina courts lacked specific jurisdiction but upheld\n    general jurisdiction over the subsidiaries because some of\n    their tires had reached North Carolina through the stream of\n    commerce\n    - Petition for writ of certiorari: Goodyear Luxembourg,\n    Goodyear Turkey, and Goodyear France (defendants below), as\n    Petitioners, sought review in the U.S. Supreme Court on the\n    grounds that North Carolina lacked adjudicatory authority\n    over them\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: Goodyear Luxembourg, Goodyear Turkey,\n    and Goodyear France (defendants below) are the petitioners;\n    the boys' parents as estate administrators (plaintiffs\n    below) are the respondents; the case arrived on certiorari\n    to address whether foreign subsidiaries of a United States\n    parent corporation are amenable to suit in state court on\n    claims unrelated to any activity of the subsidiaries in the\n    forum State\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether foreign subsidiaries of a United States parent\ncorporation are subject to general jurisdiction in a state's\ncourts, on claims unrelated to any activity of the subsidiaries\nin the forum State, because some of their products reached the\nState through the stream of commerce?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: No; the U.S. Supreme Court held that a connection so\nlimited is an inadequate basis for general jurisdiction, because\nit does not establish the \"continuous and systematic\"\naffiliation needed to make the subsidiaries at home in North\nCarolina\n- [0m[1mRule[0m:\n    - Due process limit on state-court jurisdiction: A state\n    court's exercise of jurisdiction must be compatible with the\n    Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause, which sets the\n    outer boundaries of its authority over a defendant\n    - Minimum contacts test: A State may authorize personal\n    jurisdiction over an out-of-state defendant with certain\n    minimum contacts such that the suit does not offend\n    traditional notions of fair play and substantial justice\n    - Specific jurisdiction: Case-linked jurisdiction depends on\n    an affiliation between the forum and the underlying\n    controversy, principally in-forum activity or an in-forum\n    occurrence\n    - Categories of specific jurisdiction: Specific jurisdiction\n    covers continuous and systematic in-state activity that gave\n    rise to the episode-in-suit and certain single or occasional\n    acts when the suit concerns those acts\n    - General jurisdiction at-home standard: A court may hear\n    any and all claims against a sister-state or foreign-country\n    corporation when its affiliations with the State are so\n    continuous and systematic as to render it essentially at\n    home there\n    - Paradigm forums for general jurisdiction: The paradigm\n    forum is domicile for an individual and an equivalent place\n    where a corporation is fairly regarded as at home\n    - Continuous activity insufficient for general jurisdiction:\n    Continuous activity of some sorts within a State does not by\n    itself make a corporation amenable to suits unrelated to\n    that activity\n    - Stream of commerce and general jurisdiction: Product flow\n    may bolster an affiliation germane to specific jurisdiction,\n    but ties serving that purpose do not establish general\n    jurisdiction\n    - Forum purchases and general jurisdiction: Mere forum\n    purchases, even at regular intervals, do not warrant general\n    jurisdiction over a nonresident corporation on a cause of\n    action unrelated to those purchases\n    - General jurisdiction at the center of corporate activity:\n    Due process permitted Ohio to adjudicate a claim arising\n    elsewhere against a foreign corporation whose wartime\n    business activity was conducted and supervised from Ohio\n    - Forfeiture of unraised arguments: A single-enterprise\n    theory not urged below or in the brief in opposition to\n    certiorari was forfeited and not addressed\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m:\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court first separated general\n    jurisdiction from specific jurisdiction and treated specific\n    jurisdiction as unavailable, because the accident occurred\n    in France and the tire was manufactured and sold abroad\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court reasoned that the North Carolina\n    courts' stream-of-commerce analysis \"elided the essential\n    difference between case-specific and all-purpose (general)\n    jurisdiction\"\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court explained that product flow may\n    support specific jurisdiction, but ties serving that purpose\n    do not establish general jurisdiction\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court used Perkins as the textbook\n    example of proper general jurisdiction, because the\n    corporation's wartime business activity was conducted and\n    supervised from Ohio\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court relied on Helicopteros, where a\n    CEO's negotiation trip, checks drawn on a Houston bank,\n    substantial purchases, and training trips did not support\n    general jurisdiction\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court saw no reason to treat sporadic\n    tire sales through intermediaries differently from the Texas\n    ties held insufficient in Helicopteros\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court warned that respondents' \"sprawling\n    view\" would make any substantial manufacturer or seller\n    amenable to suit on any claim wherever its products are\n    distributed\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court concluded that the subsidiaries\n    were \"in no sense at home in North Carolina\" and that their\n    \"attenuated connections\" fell far short of what general\n    jurisdiction requires\n    - The U.S. Supreme Court declined to address the\n    single-enterprise theory because respondents forfeited it\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m:\n    - The parents argued that the subsidiaries' tires reaching\n    North Carolina through the stream of commerce supported\n    general jurisdiction, the position the Court described as\n    urged by respondents and embraced by the North Carolina\n    Court of Appeals\n    - The parents belatedly argued a \"single enterprise\" theory,\n    asking the Court to consolidate the subsidiaries' North\n    Carolina ties with those of Goodyear USA and other Goodyear\n    entities, in effect piercing corporate veils for\n    jurisdictional purposes\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m:\n    - The subsidiaries maintained that North Carolina lacked\n    adjudicatory authority over them\n    - The subsidiaries stated that the type of tire involved in\n    the accident was never distributed in North Carolina\n    - The excerpt does not separately set out the rest of the\n    subsidiaries' legal arguments, though the opinion records\n    that they were unregistered and lacked places of business,\n    employees, bank accounts, advertising, solicitation, and\n    direct sales or shipments in North Carolina\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The U.S. Supreme Court reversed the judgment of\nthe North Carolina Court of Appeals\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: Goodyear teaches the central distinction between\ncase-linked and all-purpose jurisdiction, introduces the\n\"essentially at home\" formulation, and shows that\nstream-of-commerce contacts cannot be converted into general\njurisdiction; for an exam, determine first whether the claim\nconnects to the forum, then separately ask whether the defendant\nis at home there, using Perkins and Helicopteros as the\nbenchmarks\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Perkins v. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - The defendant was a Philippine mining corporation sued in\n    Ohio\n    - The corporation ceased activities in the Philippines\n    during World War II\n    - To the extent that it conducted business during and\n    immediately after the Japanese occupation, it did so in Ohio\n    - Its president maintained his office in Ohio, kept company\n    files there, and supervised \"the necessarily limited wartime\n    activities of the company\" from that office\n    - The claim-in-suit did not arise in Ohio\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Court in Ohio: the opinion does not state its ruling or\n    the procedural vehicle\n    - Movement to the U.S. Supreme Court: the opinion does not\n    state\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: the opinion does not state which party\n    was the petitioner or respondent\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether due process permitted Ohio to adjudicate a\nclaim that did not arise there against a Philippine corporation\nwhose wartime business activity was conducted and supervised\nfrom Ohio?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: Yes; the U.S. Supreme Court ruled that it would not\nviolate due process for Ohio to adjudicate the controversy\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: General jurisdiction at the center of corporate\nactivity: Due process permitted Ohio to adjudicate a claim\narising elsewhere against a foreign corporation whose wartime\nbusiness activity was conducted and supervised from Ohio\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: As described in Goodyear, the U.S. Supreme Court's\nresult rested on the fact that the corporation's business\nactivity, to the extent that any existed during the relevant\nperiod, was being conducted in Ohio under its president's\nsupervision\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthem\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthem\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The reading states that the U.S. Supreme Court\nruled that Ohio could adjudicate the controversy consistent with\ndue process, but it does not state the formal disposition\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: Goodyear calls Perkins \"the textbook case\" of\ngeneral jurisdiction over a foreign corporation that has not\nconsented to suit in the forum, so it is the main example of\ncontacts that are sufficient\n\n[0m[1mCase Name: Helicopteros Nacionales de Colombia, S.A. v. Hall[0m\n- [0m[1mDocument[0m: 1107-12\n- [0m[1mCourse[0m: Civil Procedure\n- [0m[1mFacts[0m\n    - A helicopter owned and operated by a Colombian corporation\n    crashed in Peru, killing United States citizens\n    - Their survivors brought wrongful-death actions against the\n    corporation in a Texas state court\n    - The corporation had no place of business in Texas and was\n    not licensed to do business there\n    - It sent its chief executive officer to Houston for a\n    contract-negotiation session\n    - It accepted into its New York bank account checks drawn on\n    a Houston bank\n    - It purchased helicopters, equipment, and training services\n    from a Texas enterprise for substantial sums\n    - It sent personnel to Texas for training\n    - The claim neither arose out of nor related to the\n    corporation's activities in Texas\n- [0m[1mProcedural Posture[0m:\n    - Texas state court: the opinion does not state its ruling\n    or the procedural vehicle\n    - Movement to the U.S. Supreme Court: the opinion does not\n    state\n    - U.S. Supreme Court: the opinion does not state which party\n    was the petitioner or respondent\n- [0m[1mIssue[0m: Whether a Colombian corporation's negotiation trip,\nchecks drawn on a Houston bank, purchases, and training-related\ncontacts with Texas supported general jurisdiction over\nwrongful-death claims unrelated to those contacts?\n- [0m[1mHolding[0m: No; the U.S. Supreme Court held that the\ncorporation's Texas contacts were insufficient to support\ngeneral jurisdiction, so the survivors could not maintain the\nwrongful-death actions in Texas\n- [0m[1mRule[0m: Forum purchases and general jurisdiction: Mere forum\npurchases, even at regular intervals, do not warrant general\njurisdiction over a nonresident corporation on a cause of action\nunrelated to those purchases\n- [0m[1mReasoning[0m: As described in Goodyear, the U.S. Supreme Court\nreasoned that the links to Texas did not \"constitute the kind of\ncontinuous and systematic general business contacts\" found in\nPerkins and could not support jurisdiction over a claim\nunrelated to the Texas activities\n- [0m[1mPlaintiff's/Appellee's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthem\n- [0m[1mDefendant/Appellant's Arguments[0m: The reading does not state\nthem\n- [0m[1mDisposition[0m: The reading states that the U.S. Supreme Court\nheld the survivors could not maintain their wrongful-death\nactions in Texas, but it does not state the formal disposition\n- [0m[1mImportance[0m: Helicopteros is the counterpart to Perkins and\nshows that substantial, repeated commercial dealings with a\nforum may still fall short of general jurisdiction; Goodyear\nextends its logic to sporadic product sales through\nintermediaries\n[0m[1m4. Definitions[0m\n[0m[1mPersonal jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A court's authority to proceed against a\nparticular defendant, subject to the limits of the Fourteenth\nAmendment's Due Process Clause in the state-court setting\ndescribed in the reading\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The subsidiaries contested North Carolina's\nauthority over them, and the opinion analyzes that challenge\nthrough general and specific jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is the broader concept; general and specific\njurisdiction are the two categories discussed in this reading\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mGeneral jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: All-purpose jurisdiction allowing a court to hear\nany and all claims against a defendant, including claims\nunrelated to its forum contacts, when the defendant is\nessentially at home in the forum\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: This was the only basis left in Goodyear after\nNorth Carolina's lack of specific jurisdiction was acknowledged,\nand the Court held it unavailable\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: General jurisdiction turns on the defendant's\noverall affiliation with the forum, while specific jurisdiction\nturns on the connection between the forum and the controversy\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown; Perkins\nv. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.; Helicopteros Nacionales de\nColombia, S.A. v. Hall\n\n[0m[1mSpecific jurisdiction[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: Case-linked jurisdiction based on an affiliation\nbetween the forum and the underlying controversy, principally\nactivity or an occurrence in the forum State\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It was unavailable in Goodyear because the\naccident occurred in France and the tire was manufactured and\nsold abroad\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It reaches claims connected to the forum\ncontacts, not all claims against the defendant\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mMinimum contacts[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The defendant's contacts with a State that are\nsufficient for maintaining the suit not to offend traditional\nnotions of fair play and substantial justice\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: This is the International Shoe standard from\nwhich the opinion's jurisdictional categories develop\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Minimum contacts is the overarching test, while\n\"at home\" is the more demanding description of the affiliation\nrequired for general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mFair play and substantial justice[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The due process concept that the maintenance of a\nsuit must not offend traditional notions of fairness and\njustice, as expressed in International Shoe\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The Court says International Shoe classified\nout-of-state corporate cases in an effort to give this concept\nspecific content\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It is paired with minimum contacts in the test\nand is not presented as a separate category of jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mContinuous and systematic[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: A description of corporate activity or affiliation\nwith a forum that is ongoing and regular; for general\njurisdiction, the affiliation must be strong enough to render\nthe corporation essentially at home\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The subsidiaries' limited North Carolina\nconnections did not meet this standard for general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: The phrase can describe activity supporting\nspecific jurisdiction when that activity gave rise to the suit,\nso its presence alone does not establish general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown;\nHelicopteros Nacionales de Colombia, S.A. v. Hall\n\n[0m[1mAt home[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The status of a defendant whose affiliation with a\nforum is comparable to an individual's domicile, so that the\nforum may exercise general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the key formulation in Goodyear; the\nsubsidiaries were \"in no sense at home\" in North Carolina\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Being at home differs from merely doing some\nbusiness or having products reach the forum\n- [0m[1mCase",
        "[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown; Perkins\nv. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.\n\n[0m[1mDomicile[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: The reading does not define the term beyond\nidentifying it as the paradigm forum for general jurisdiction\nover an individual\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It is the reference point for identifying an\nequivalent place where a corporation is at home\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: Domicile is the individual paradigm, while the\ncorporate analogue is the place where the corporation is fairly\nregarded as at home\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mForeign corporation[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: As used in the jurisdictional discussion, a\ncorporation from a sister State or a foreign country\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The term shows that the opinion's\ngeneral-jurisdiction standard is not limited to corporations\nfrom other countries\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: \"Foreign\" in this context does not necessarily\nmean from outside the United States, and the Court also uses\n\"foreign subsidiaries\" for the entities organized abroad\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown; Perkins\nv. Benguet Consol. Mining Co.\n\n[0m[1mStream of commerce[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: As used in the reading, the flow of a\nmanufacturer's products into the forum, including through\nintermediaries\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: The North Carolina Court of Appeals relied on it\nfor general jurisdiction, and the Supreme Court rejected that\nreasoning\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: It may bolster an affiliation germane to specific\njurisdiction but does not establish that a defendant is at home\nfor general jurisdiction\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mPurposeful availment[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: An act by which a defendant purposefully avails\nitself of the privilege of conducting activities in the forum,\ninvoking the