ns attributing one entity's\ncontacts to another, while stream of commerce concerns products\nreaching the forum\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n\n[0m[1mForfeiture[0m\n- [0m[1mDefinition[0m: As applied in the reading, the loss of a\ncontention because a party did not urge it below or in its brief\nin opposition to the petition for certiorari\n- [0m[1mSignificance[0m: It disposed of the single-enterprise theory\nwithout a decision on the merits\n- [0m[1mDistinction[0m: A forfeited argument is not addressed, which\ndiffers from an argument that a court considers and rejects\n- [0m[1mCase[0m: Goodyear Dunlop Tires Operations, S.A. v. Brown\n[0m[1mGaps in the Attached Reading[0m\n- International Shoe and Hanson are not briefed because the\nreading uses them for rules and gives no facts about their\ndisputes.\n- The excerpt omits parts of the opinion marked by asterisks and\ndoes not reproduce the text of footnote 5.\n- The excerpt does not mention any North Carolina Supreme Court\nstep or state the procedural vehicle for the trial court's\njurisdictional ruling. The posture above includes only what the\nexcerpt supports, and the appeal to the Court of Appeals is\ninferred from the subsidiaries' jurisdictional challenge and the\nCourt's discussion of the North Carolina courts' rulings.\n"
